From 3d19630898c025477e63326c684eeef92330d821 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: halt95 <halt95@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Tue, 15 Sep 2026 10:38:55 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 56/76] v2-ppfix2: close the three qualification-test gaps
 Astra R2 found (tests only)

Astra round 2 returned MERGE-READY for the production change, with three
corrections owed on the GPU suite before its coverage claims can be credited.
No vllm/ file changes in this commit -- tests only.

1. THE SELF-COPY RESET WAS UNOBSERVABLE. The test seeded num_accepted = 1 and
   then asserted 1, so deleting the reset store at mamba_utils.py:473-475 would
   have passed. Reseeded to (num_accepted=2, state_idx=1,
   new_num_computed=33): aligned=32, running=32, needs_copy true, bias=0,
   dst_col=1, src_col=1 -- same early-exit shape, but the 2 -> 1 store is now
   the thing being measured.

   Astra also noted that unchanged bytes cannot distinguish an early exit from
   an identical self-copy. They cannot, and the docstring now says so instead of
   implying otherwise. What can be made observable without touching the kernel
   is the *conjunction*: a new case seeds (num_accepted=4, state_idx=1,
   new_num_computed=33) -> bias=2 with src_block_idx still == dest_block_idx, so
   the guard must NOT fire. conv takes the same-block memmove branch
   (mamba_utils.py:320-336) and temporal reads src_col + bias = column 3, a
   different physical block, so both are observable. Same column with zero bias
   moves nothing; same column with positive bias moves bytes.

2. THE FREED-SLOT TEST DID NOT ESTABLISH GUARD SENSITIVITY. Astra is right that
   a hand-written -1 has no connection to decisions seeded at some other slot:
   removing the guard would read *before* the decision arrays, not at slot 3.
   Two replacements.

   (a) Poison where a missing guard would actually look. The block tables and
   every per-req decision array are now views one row/element into a backing
   tensor, so pointer index -1 lands on real memory this test owns. Index -1 of
   the decision arrays holds (num_accepted=4, state_idx=0,
   new_num_computed=48) -- a copy with bias 3 into dst_col 2 -- and block-table
   row -1 points column 2 at CANARY_DST and everything else at CANARY_SRC. A
   kernel that failed to reject req_idx < 0 would therefore write CANARY_DST
   from CANARY_SRC, and both canaries are inside the "every other block is
   unchanged" sweep.

   (b) Exercise the real masking. `mask_from_generation_change` in the CPU
   sibling drives `PPHandler.get_prev_sampled_outputs` (pp_utils.py:132-140)
   with a slot whose generation counter moved between receive and consumption,
   and returns the mapping the handler computes; a new CPU test pins
   [5,1,2] + reuse at row 1 -> [5,-1,2], both rows reused -> [-1,1,-1], and no
   reuse -> unchanged. The GPU suite then feeds that produced mapping to the
   kernel, with the reused slot carrying copy-shaped decisions.

   (c) Rejection before any negative-index load, verified on CPU without a
   device: a new test parses the kernel with ast and asserts that the
   `if req_idx < 0: return` guard precedes every `<ptr> + req_idx` expression.
   Non-vacuous on this tree -- guard at kernel-relative line 81, seven
   req_idx-indexed accesses at 87-115.

3. INTEGER-VIEW COMPARISONS. Every "bit-identical" claim now goes through
   `_assert_bitwise`, which compares the int16 view with torch.equal. A
   zero-tolerance float compare establishes numeric equality, not bit identity:
   +0.0 == -0.0 and NaN != NaN. These are byte copies, so the integer view is
   the right instrument. No torch.testing.assert_close remains in the file.

Astra's two qualifications on round 2's wording, recorded: "unconditional"
means every *dispatched* align kernel -- the zero-request and uninitialised
early returns at the top of `run_fused_postprocess_align` remain, so it is not
every method invocation; and "mutates nothing" applies to the failing launcher
itself, not to the caller, which can already have scattered acceptance counts
via `postprocess_state` before reaching it.

A process correction, since it changes what earlier test reports meant. Rounds
1 and 2 described `tests/v1/worker/test_mamba_utils.py`,
`test_mamba_hybrid_model_state.py` and `test_gpu_block_table.py` as CPU suites.
They are not: `TestPostprocessMambaFusedKernel` is gated on
`torch.cuda.is_available()` and the other two on `current_platform.is_cuda()`,
both true on the reference host, so those tests executed on the GPU in every earlier run.
The round-2 report disclosed six accidental GPU tests; the real number was
larger. Every run in this commit sets CUDA_VISIBLE_DEVICES="" so
`torch.cuda.is_available()` is False and nothing can reach a device, and
`test_gpu_block_table.py` (module-gated on the platform rather than the device,
and unrelated to this change) is excluded rather than run.

Test runs (CUDA_VISIBLE_DEVICES="", the reference host):
  tests/v1/worker/gpu ................................ 9 passed, 8 skipped
  + test_mamba_utils + test_pp_utils
  + test_mamba_hybrid_model_state (2 CUDA tests deselected)
                                     ................ 23 passed, 43 skipped

The 43 skips are the CUDA-gated tests this mode correctly refuses to run. No
vllm/ file changed, so the heavier suites (tests/v1/ple_offload,
worker -k "pp or mamba") are unchanged from the previous commit and were not
re-run under a busy box.

Still owed, unchanged from Astra R2: a deliberate isolated run of the GPU suite
under VLLM_E2_GPU_TESTS=1, multi-group coverage, PCP, CUDA-graph operation, the
block-lifetime investigation, the probe boot pair, and V.3/V.7 re-qualification.
The GPU suite in this commit has not been executed in any form.
